= Spotlight effect =

== Overview ==

{{:Spotlight effect/Simple}}

The '''spotlight effect''' concerns overestimation of the degree to which other observers notice or attend to a person's behavior or appearance. It is a defined phenomenon in experimental social judgment, not a statement that others never pay attention.

== Key research structure ==

Researchers can ask a participant to predict whether a stimulus or behavior will be noticed and then compare this prediction with observers' recorded responses. The gap between predicted and actual noticeability constitutes the primary dependent variable; the result depends on stimulus conspicuousness, opportunity to observe, social context and measurement method.

Gilovich, Medvec and Savitsky (2000) conducted original experiments demonstrating overestimation in particular situations and explored anchoring-and-adjustment explanations. Review work distinguishes this bias from the illusion of transparency, which concerns imagined visibility of internal states.<ref name="gilovich_core">Thomas Gilovich, Victoria H. Medvec and Kenneth Savitsky, [https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.78.2.211 “The Spotlight Effect in Social Judgment”], ''Journal of Personality and Social Psychology'' 78(2), 211–222 (2000).</ref><ref name="review_core">Thomas Gilovich and Kenneth Savitsky, [https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-8721.00039 “The Spotlight Effect and the Illusion of Transparency”], ''Current Directions in Psychological Science'' 8(6) (1999).</ref>

== Measurement and constraints ==

A valid claim requires a consistent denominator of observers, independent measurement of actual noticing and analysis of variability, including whether participants were exposed to different stimulus salience. Individual-level prediction error can differ from group-level mean bias; the phenomenon need not occur for every participant.

The bias belongs to [[Related to::Cognition|cognitive processes]], [[Related to::Metacognition|self-evaluation]] and [[Related to::Reasoning|social judgment]]. It may also relate descriptively to [[Related to::Mental simulation|imagining what others perceive]], but there is no proven universal identity between those mechanisms.

== Limits and generalization ==

The original experimental contexts do not license a single percent for all cultures, ages, occupations or social settings. A highly salient or socially relevant action may be noticed as much as or more than predicted. If only participants who remember a mistake are sampled, the study may also suffer selection bias.

The phenomenon is not a clinical diagnosis of social anxiety, nor a reason to disregard genuine observer feedback. Advanced research should disentangle anchoring, memory bias, social attention and measurement artifacts.

== Semantic relationships ==

The spotlight effect relates to [[Related to::Cognition|cognition]], [[Related to::Metacognition|self-evaluation]], [[Related to::Reasoning|social judgment]] and [[Related to::Mental simulation|imagining others' perceptions]]. These are associations, not demonstrated mechanistic equivalences.

